(vec {
  "\"Good game, m'lord. Would you like to play again?\"";
  "\"Bewildering beast. You are unaffected,\" the king spoke as he picked up the wizard hat and placed it back on Jalca's head.\n\"Carry me to the board, sire, so we may play another\".";
  "\"I told you\", the young prince exclaimed as he tugged on his mother's dress. \"Papa's talking to himself again\".\n\"My dearest\", the queen called out from the entrance to the thrown hall. \"Is everything alright?\"\n\n\"Of course!\" The replied, fixing Jalca's head to his body. \"Do you see how stupendous it is that our modest fool never disclosed his ability to lose his head and not die? Fascinating.\"";
  "\"Let me fix you another evening nightcap, dear,\" the queen remarked, turning to beckon the scullery maid. \"Oh, Jocelyn! Could you come here please?\"\n\n\"I know what you're trying to do!\" The king squinted and pointed his finger. \n\"As always,\" Jalca sighed, massaging his neck wound. \"Put you to sleep then hide your chess pieces.\"\n\n\"Mommy?\"\n\"Go to bed, little one,\" the queen bent down to hug her child whilst keeping eye on her husband. Hearing Jocelyn approach, she quickly turned, \"Call Brucey and Charles.\"\n\n\"Right, ma'am. Right away.\"\n\n\"Not so fast!\" The king said as he grabbed his sword, pointing it at the entrance where his wife stood, and their guards Brucey and Charles would soon arrive. \"Jalca, get all the pieces together. We'll make a daring escape\".\n\n\"I'm leading the way, my king,\" Jalca called back, hanging out the window, elbows on the sill. \"Get the chessboard and pieces yourself, and follow me.\"\n\nThe king gasped, \" You scoundrel! I will skewer you for running out like this!\"\n\nAs Brucey and Charles heaved into the thrown room, the king had secured the chessboard (pieces inside) under his left arm and swiped the air with his sword while hopping backwards to the window. \"Please, m'lord,\" Charles begged, moving slowly. \"Can we not just have our nightcap and get to bed?\"\n\n\"You two are lucky I don't cut open your bellies,\" the king said, placing one leg out of the window. He then looked back at his wife, \"Mind your belly, too, my dear! When I'm back after safekeeping my little chess game, I'm coming for all your bellies.\"";
  "Following a few leaps, drops and landings, the king feet the outside grass beneath his shoeless feet and heard his wife, \"... before he gets himself lost! Hurry!\"\n\n\"Jalca, where are you?\" \n\n\"Look who it is, m'lord,\" Jalca's voice asked, though the king had to turn around to determine where.\n\nFrom the bush emerged Jocelyn, \"I believe you, my king. It was never my desire to feed you those drowsy cups.\"\n\n\"How did you get down here so quickly? Faster than me?\"";
  "\"Mayhaps she is magic like me,\" the fool's voice fluttered about.\n\"Where are you?!\"\n\n\"Look on thy shoulder, m'lord,\" Jocelyn pointed out the diminutive figure standing on the king's shoulder.\nNeeding to crank his head, doing so wildly, \"My god, Jalca!!\"\n\nThe king grabbed Jalca by the head and held him aloft, \"You are a strange creature..\"";
  "\"We should probably leave quickly,\" Jocelyn expressed. \"You're acting funny again, and Brucey and Charles are surely on their way here.\"\n\n\"You were drugging me, wench!\" \n\n\"Orders are orders, sore,\" Jalca chimed in.\n\n\"I might be just a servant in your employment, but I wasn't always.\"\n\n\"Why should I trust you? Either of you?!\"\n\n\"Calm down, m'lord. Take a deep...\"";
  "Enraged, the king squeezed the fool's head, causing Jocelyn to exclaim \"Oh my!\"\nJalca shrieked and, after much frantic wrestling, managed to pluck his head from the king's grasp.\nScrambling up the arm and down the inside of the king's robe, Jalca disappeared!\n\n\"Where did you go?!\"";
  "\"We really should go. I'm here to help you\"\n\n\"He's gone,\" the king said, patting himself down. \"Alright. We'll go. But I'm not following you, you're following me.\"\n\"This way?\" Jocelyn asked, pointing behind her, deeper into the forest.\n\"Correct, let's move before I am forced to kill my disloyal guards.\"";
  "Dodging increasing number of trees as they traversed the thickening shrubbery, the king's mighty sword became more difficult to carry.\n\"This is, enough. I've had enough,\" the king panted for Jocelyn to stop.\n\"Very well, m'lord. I think we should be safe here.\"\n\"I need some water. Fetch me some, maid servant.\"\n\"Um, alright,\" Jocelyn replied, hoping to hear a nearby stream.\n\n\"Let's set-up a game while we wait,\" the familiar voice suggested.\n\nAstounded, the king whipped his head around to find Jalca on king-piggyback! \"Get off me!\"\n\nJalca did as instructed very quickly, slipping the king's chessboard into his possession as he did, and proceeded to set up pieces in a small clearing. \n\n\"I'll find us some water. Give me some time.\"\n\"Very well...\"";
  "All three seated, each with a mug of river water, the king considered his first move.\n\n\"Move your knight, m'lord,\" jalca suggested.\n\n\"You're not playing,\" the king snapped while still evaluating the standard opening.\n\n\"Not playing?! Of course I must play!\"\n\n\"Quiet!\" The king waved his hand at the fool wizard. \"Play on the servant's team if you must.\"\n\n\"I'm not aware of who you speak of, my king,\" Jocelyn countered. \"But I must insist that you acknowledge that I was, and still am, the queen of Rookshire.\"\n\nThe king quietly moved a flank pawn two spaces. \"What are you talking about?\"\n\n\"I'm not a common servant. Your wife took me into service after your forces ambushed our beautiful citadel. I'm a royal.\"\n\n\"Ambushed you, ey?\" The king guffawed. \"A surprise attack, you didn't see coming!\"\n\n\"Well, surprises beget more surprises,\" Jocelyn warned, taking a rook piece from her sleeve and placing it in the center of the board.\"\n\nThe king's eyes shot wide as jalca said, \"She has the magic touch!\"";
  "Back at the castle, the queen welcomed their in-house bishop. \"Dear Percy, it is as you feared and warned us for some time...\"";
})
